#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087491 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d82c39c-5ec6-5cf3-96f8-e778091a451a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions Containing Natural Logarithms Using a Calculator"}] :question/id #uuid "effc520d-4717-5e46-9358-c2ea34c7aa24" :question/math-academy-id "e-3990" :question/problem "Rounded to $2$ decimal places, what is the value of $\\dfrac{4}{\\ln(2)}$?" :question/worked-solution "We compute the required expression to $2$ decimal places using a calculator, rounding the intermediate steps to $5$ decimal places to avoid rounding error. This gives\n\n$$\n(4)/(\\ln (2)) \\mid \\approx \\frac{4}{0.69315}; \\approx 5.77076; \\approx 5.77\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087492 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30497207-86c5-507f-906a-d23bd0af71e9" :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Exponential and Logarithmic Form With Natural Logarithms"}] :question/id #uuid "97f9f0d6-d948-5f9b-b0ae-57e6a456a53b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4056" :question/problem "Convert the expression $\\ln{3} \\approx 1.1$ to its equivalent exponential form." :question/worked-solution "The natural logarithm of $a$ is defined as follows:\n\n> $\\ln{a} = x$ is equivalent to $a = e^x$\n\nIf we compare\n\n$$\n\\ln{3} \\approx 1.1\n$$\n\nwith $\\ln{a} = x$, we get $a = 3$ and $x = 1.1$.\n\nTherefore, the approximation\n\n$$\n\\ln{3} \\approx 1.1\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\ne^{1.1} \\approx 3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087493 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087685 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a323ad55-d029-58f4-b0a3-cc10b7dab125" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Natural Logarithms"}] :question/id #uuid "fd02e00f-9e2c-54f1-a5d3-fb0da9c9a413" :question/math-academy-id "e-4057" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\ln\\left(e^{6}\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "The natural logarithm of a power of $e$ is the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\ln(e^x) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x = 6$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\ln\\left(e^{6}\\right) = 6\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087497 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087692 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "169ca81a-c698-553c-9f47-d6c4303b82a9" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Natural Logarithms of Fractions and Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "37ca3b87-9f5f-583c-95ff-83ca35608559" :question/math-academy-id "e-4155" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1}{e^{3}}\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{e^3}\n$$\n\nas a power of $e$ (the base of the natural logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{e^3} = e^{-3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1}{e^3} \\right) = \\ln \\left(e^{-3} \\right)\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm of a power of $e$ is the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\ln(e^x) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x = -3$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\ln\\left(e^{-3}\\right) =-3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088088 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d82c39c-5ec6-5cf3-96f8-e778091a451a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions Containing Natural Logarithms Using a Calculator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9de93595-3e7b-5b56-a3a0-fa1b9c20ad4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.792" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085196} {:answer/id #uuid "3549bd05-2f37-5e33-94f8-6d58ba6447ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.608" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085197} {:answer/id #uuid "3dc8d463-7185-51d0-8829-e07324901f9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.609" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085198} {:answer/id #uuid "44ac2971-af63-59dc-aa4b-b699e3a4f55e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "